  "content": "The user is asking about the Singleton pattern in C#. This is a well-known design pattern. I should explain what it is, why it's used, and provide a clear code example. Let me cover:\n\n1. Definition\n2. Why it's used\n3. A standard implementation (thread-safe)\n4. Brief pros/cons or considerations\n\nI'll keep it clear and practical with a code example.\n</think>\n\n# Singleton Pattern in C#\n\nA **Singleton** is a design pattern that ensures a class has **exactly one instance** and provides a global point of access to it.\n\n## Why use it?\n\n- You need a single, shared instance (e.g., a configuration manager, a logger, a database connection pool).\n- You want to prevent accidental creation of multiple instances.\n\n## Classic Implementation (Thread-Safe)\n\n```csharp\npublic sealed class Singleton\n{\n    private static readonly Singleton _instance = new Singleton();\n\n    // Private constructor prevents external instantiation\n    private Singleton() { }\n\n    // Global access point\n    public static Singleton Instance => _instance;\n\n    // Example method\n    public void DoWork()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Singleton is doing work.\");\n",
